    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nStared unpack error\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\nimport torch\n\ndef foo(x, y):\n    a, b, c = *x.shape[:2], y.shape[0]\n    return a + b + c\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    x = torch.zeros([2, 3], device=\"cuda\")\n    y = torch.zeros([4, 5], device=\"cuda\")\n    result = foo(x, y)\n    print(result)\n```\n\nerror:\n```\ncythonize -i x.py \n\nCompiling /home/x.py because it changed.\n[1/1] Cythonizing /home/x.py\n\nError compiling Cython file:\n------------------------------------------------------------\n...\n\nimport torch\n\n\ndef foo(x, y):\n    a, b, c = *x.shape[:2], y.shape[0]\n    ^\n------------------------------------------------------------\nx.py:7:4: need more than 2 values to unpack\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nI want to know:\n\n1. The reason for the failure in the example above\n\n2. Why modifying it to `a, b = x.shape[:2]` is feasible\n\n3. Is there a way to solve this problem without modifying the source code of foo\n\n### OS\n\nLinux\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.2.4\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
